{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. I understand you’ve been having nausea and vomiting again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, since yesterday morning. I’ve thrown up over ten times—greenish stuff, not bloody. I couldn’t keep anything down except a little ginger ale in the ER."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds really tough. Any pain, fever, or diarrhea?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No pain or fever, but I feel chills and so tired. It’s like my usual gastroparesis flare."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Your vitals show you’re a bit tachycardic and your blood pressure was high earlier, but it’s come down. We’ll start IV fluids and some anti-nausea meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I took a compazine suppository at home—it helped a little."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good to know. We gave you IV compazine, Zofran, and fluids in the ER. You also got insulin since your blood sugar was high."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "How’s my blood sugar now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s stable. We adjusted your insulin while you’re not eating. You’ll continue your usual Lantus at night and resume your sliding scale when you can eat."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:27:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, good. I’ve had three admissions this month—worried something’s wrong with my gastric pacer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see you had it checked recently. Since then, you’ve had two more flares. Dr. Patel said unless things worsen, we’ll keep your current plan. But you can stop by his clinic any afternoon if you’re concerned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s reassuring. What about my blood pressure? It’s been all over the place."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s been labile. You were on clonidine before but stopped. We’re restarting a clonidine patch—once a week. It’s better for you since you vomit so often."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I forgot to pick up the prescription last time. Will I get it this time?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re sending it to your pharmacy. But you’ll need to follow up with your PCP to keep it going. We’re also adjusting your lisinopril to 7.5 mg daily."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. My white blood cell count was high—should I worry?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It was 15.8, but no fever or infection signs. Likely from dehydration and stress. It’s already down to 7.0 today—back to normal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. What about my stomach? Any blockage?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a chest X-ray—no obstruction or free air. Lungs are clear. Your gastric pacer leads are in place. So no signs of perforation or blockage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I’ve been on Reglan at home—will I keep that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2197-05-11 23:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re giving IV Reglan every six hours. Once you can eat, we’ll switch back to oral—same dose, four times a day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2197-05-12 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2197-05-12 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. I haven’t vomited since last night. I sipped some broth—kept it down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2197-05-12 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great progress. We’ll try advancing your diet to soft foods today—like toast and applesauce. Small amounts, slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2197-05-12 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I have ginger ale? Regular, not diet?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2197-05-12 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, regular ginger ale is fine. In fact, if you’re only tolerating clear liquids at home, it’s better to have regular ones to avoid ketosis, especially with your diabetes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2197-05-12 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t know that. I’ve been sticking to diet drinks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2197-05-12 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "With your history, if you’re not eating enough, your body can go into ketosis. So regular clear liquids are okay short-term. But resume your diabetic diet when you can."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well today—kept down soup, crackers, and juice. We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow morning if this continues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. What meds will I go home with?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Same as before: Protonix for GERD, Reglan four times a day, aspirin, and your insulin. Plus, we’re adding the clonidine patch for blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And lisinopril?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, now 7.5 mg daily. But we’re writing it as 5 mg tablets—you’ll take one and a half each day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Any follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You have an appointment with Dr. Patel in two weeks for your pacer. If symptoms worsen before then, you can walk in to his clinic any afternoon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What if I start vomiting again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2197-05-12 14:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Go back to clear liquids. If you can only keep those down, it’s okay to have regular ones to prevent ketosis. But if you develop fever, chest pain, or trouble breathing, come back right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve had no vomiting overnight and ate a light breakfast. We’re clearing you for discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. Just to confirm—I’m taking the clonidine patch weekly, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, one patch weekly. And keep all your other meds. Follow up with your PCP about the patch and your blood pressure control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if I feel chills or fatigue again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Monitor your symptoms. Fatigue and chills can be part of a flare. But if you spike a fever or feel worse, call your doctor or return here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers and prescriptions ready. You should be out by 10 a.m."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One last thing—my urine test showed ketones earlier, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s why we emphasized regular clear liquids. It helps prevent your body from breaking down fat for energy when you’re not eating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. I’ll watch that at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. You’re doing great. Just take it slow with food and keep up with your follow-ups."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will do. Thanks again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2197-05-13 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. We’ll see you in clinic if needed, but hopefully not in the hospital again soon."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2197-05-11 23:18:00",
    "admission_end": "2197-05-13 16:23:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 30-year-old woman with Type 1 diabetes, gastroparesis s/p gastric pacer, and hypertension was admitted for the third time in a month due to nausea and recurrent bilious vomiting, consistent with a gastroparesis flare. She presented unable to tolerate oral intake except for ginger ale and had been using compazine at home with partial relief. She was treated with IV fluids, antiemetics including Reglan and compazine, and insulin adjustment. Her diet was advanced to soft foods by the second hospital day as symptoms improved. Leukocytosis on admission resolved without evidence of infection, attributed to dehydration and stress. Blood pressure was labile; she was restarted on a clonidine patch and continued on lisinopril. She was discharged home in stable condition tolerating oral intake, with instructions to resume all prior medications and follow up with her gastroenterologist and PCP.",
    "problems": [
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type I [juvenile type], not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Esophagitis, unspecified",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Leukocytosis, unspecified"
    ]
  }
}
